description:
Add a thin helper that handles the AEAD encryption details for individual DB column values. This sits between the UserKeyStore (which provides the DEK) and the data access layer (which stores bytes).

## Why AES-256-GCM
  Authenticated encryption: provides both confidentiality AND integrity.
  A tampered ciphertext returns an error, not garbage plaintext.
  Standard, well-audited, supported by the RustCrypto aes-gcm crate.

## Crate to add
  aes-gcm = { version = "0.10", features = ["aes"] }
  (RustCrypto — consistent with the existing use of sha2, rsa from the same family)

## FieldCipher (crates/mail/src/crypto/field_cipher.rs)

  pub struct FieldCipher {
      dek: [u8; 32],  // plaintext DEK, must not outlive the request
  }

  impl FieldCipher {
      pub fn new(dek: [u8; 32]) -> Self;

      /// Encrypt a plaintext byte slice. Returns: 12-byte nonce || ciphertext || 16-byte tag.
      /// A fresh random nonce is generated per call — two encryptions of the same plaintext
      /// produce different ciphertexts. This is correct and required.
      pub fn encrypt(&self, plaintext: &[u8]) -> Result<Vec<u8>, CipherError>;

      /// Decrypt a ciphertext produced by encrypt(). Returns plaintext or error.
      /// Error if ciphertext is truncated, nonce is reused, or tag fails verification.
      pub fn decrypt(&self, ciphertext: &[u8]) -> Result<Vec<u8>, CipherError>;

      /// Convenience: encrypt a UTF-8 string, return bytes.
      pub fn encrypt_str(&self, s: &str) -> Result<Vec<u8>, CipherError> {
          self.encrypt(s.as_bytes())
      }

      /// Convenience: decrypt bytes, return UTF-8 string.
      pub fn decrypt_str(&self, ciphertext: &[u8]) -> Result<String, CipherError>;
  }

  Wire format: [ nonce (12 bytes) | ciphertext (variable) | GCM tag (16 bytes) ]
  Minimum valid ciphertext length: 28 bytes (12 + 0 + 16).

## Test vectors
  Tests must NOT use encrypt-then-decrypt-with-same-code as the oracle.
  Instead: include at least one test with a hardcoded DEK and known plaintext
  that verifies the output matches a reference computed with OpenSSL:
    openssl enc -aes-256-gcm -K <hex-dek> -iv <hex-nonce> -in plaintext.bin

## Acceptance
  - encrypt → decrypt roundtrip returns original plaintext
  - Flipping any byte in the ciphertext causes decrypt to return Err (not wrong plaintext)
  - Empty plaintext encrypts and decrypts correctly
  - At least one test vector from OpenSSL cross-validation
